+This Definition restricts comparisons $A\prec B$ to $m(A)=m(B)$; the coefficient-map supplier defines the mass, not the comparison relation. Write $A\sim B$ when both directions hold, and $A\prec\prec B$ when only the forward direction holds. On any fixed simple type, dividing every represented state by the same positive constant $w_\Gamma$ gives its unit-point-mass comparison. Positive scaling and its inverse preserve and reflect order; whenever the required CH and reference hypotheses hold, the unit-state entropy theorem applies after that normalization. For a fixed simple type $\Gamma$, let $S:\Gamma\to\mathbb R$ be an **additive compound representation on its raw unit tags**: for every pair $C=\sum_j a_j e_{(\Gamma,x_j)}$ and $D=\sum_k b_k e_{(\Gamma,y_k)}$ with finite nonnegative coefficients and $\sum_j a_j=\sum_k b_k$, require $C\prec D$ if and only if $\sum_j a_jS(x_j)\le\sum_k b_kS(y_k)$. Empty sums have value zero. Then the entropy of a represented state is $w_\Gamma S(x)$ and the entropy of a finite sum $\sum_j t_j\mathsf{J}_\Gamma(x_j)$ is $\sum_j t_j w_\Gamma S(x_j)$. Representing only singleton unit-state comparisons is insufficient for this additive formula. No independent rescaling across different types is assumed without a calibrator.
 
 The common relation satisfies A1–A6 on all equal-mass finite compounds: (A1) reflexivity; (A2) transitivity; (A3) if $A\prec B$, then $A+C\prec B+C$ for every compound $C$; (A4) if $A\prec B$, then $tA\prec tB$ for every $t>0$; (A5) $t\mathsf{J}_\Gamma(x)$ is equivalent to $s\mathsf{J}_\Gamma(x)+(t-s)\mathsf{J}_\Gamma(x)$ for $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, with the reversible scaled-copy transport above; (A6) for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive equal-mass spectators $Z_0,Z_1$, and every sequence $\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to zero, $A+\epsilon_n Z_0\prec B+\epsilon_n Z_1$ for all $n$ implies $A\prec B$. These are hypotheses, not conclusions. No comparison hypothesis is included here.
 
